= Unit 8 · Noetherian methods and applications

*Bridge into this unit.* Noetherianity converts indefinitely growing choices into finite generating lists. Hilbert's proof needs two such lists: one controls high-degree leading terms, and the other handles a bounded-degree remainder. Track the dependence of this argument on the module finiteness theorem.

== Lecture 15 · Finiteness conditions and Hilbert's basis theorem
#focus[Prove equivalent Noetherian conditions, transfer them through exact sequences, and prove Hilbert's basis theorem.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall a submodule of a finite free module over a Noetherian ring is finitely generated.


#definition[Noetherian module][A module is Noetherian if every increasing chain of submodules stabilizes. A commutative ring is Noetherian if it is Noetherian as a module over itself, so the chains in question are chains of ideals.]

#strategy[Replace a chain condition by finite generation, then lower polynomial degree using finitely many leading coefficients.]

#theorem[Equivalent finiteness condition][A module is Noetherian exactly when every submodule is finitely generated.]
#proof[If every submodule is finitely generated, the union of an increasing chain is generated by finitely many elements, all lying in one chain member; the chain stabilizes there. Conversely, if a submodule were not finitely generated, successively choose an element outside the submodule generated by all previous choices. This produces a strictly increasing chain, contradicting the ascending chain condition.]

#theorem[Exact-sequence criterion][In $0 -> A -> B -> C ->0$, the module $B$ is Noetherian exactly when both $A,C$ are Noetherian. Consequently every finitely generated module over a Noetherian ring is Noetherian.]
#proof[Submodules of a Noetherian module are Noetherian, and submodule correspondence proves the same for quotients. Conversely for a submodule $N$ of $B$, the intersection with $A$ and its image in $C$ are finitely generated. Lift a finite generating set of the image to $N$ and combine it with generators of the intersection. Subtracting the lifted image expression from any element of $N$ leaves an element of the intersection, so these finitely many elements generate $N$. Finite direct sums of $R$ are therefore Noetherian by induction, and every finitely generated module is their quotient.]

#theorem[Hilbert's basis theorem][If $R$ is Noetherian, then $R[x]$ is Noetherian. Hence every polynomial ring in finitely many variables over $R$ is Noetherian.]
#strategy[Choose finitely many leading coefficients, reduce high degrees, and handle the bounded-degree remainder as a submodule of a finite free module.]
#proof[Let $I$ be an ideal of $R[x]$. The set of leading coefficients of its nonzero polynomials, together with zero, is an ideal: align degrees by multiplying by powers of $x$ before adding, and scale coefficients by ring elements. Choose generators $a_i$ of this coefficient ideal and corresponding $f_i in I$ of degrees $d_i$. If $I=0$, there is nothing to prove. Otherwise put $N=max_i d_i$. The polynomials of degree less than $N$ form a finite free $R$-module, so its intersection with $I$ is generated as an $R$-module by finitely many $g_j$. For $f in I$ of degree $d>=N$, express its leading coefficient as $sum_i r_i a_i$ and subtract $sum_i r_i x^(d-d_i)f_i$. The degree strictly decreases. Induction reduces to degree less than $N$, where the $g_j$ suffice. Thus the $f_i,g_j$ generate $I$ as an ideal. Induct on the number of variables.]

#example[Why the bounded-degree remainder cannot be skipped][Choosing polynomials whose leading coefficients generate the coefficient ideal lets us reduce any sufficiently high degree. It does not necessarily express a low-degree remainder using those same polynomials, because multiplying by a negative power of $x$ is not allowed. The proof therefore collects the ideal's degree-less-than-$N$ part as a submodule of $R^N$ and supplies separate finite generators for it.]

This is the logical purpose of the two finite lists in Hilbert's proof. A degree-lowering algorithm without a base case is not a completed induction. When $N=0$, the bounded-degree part is zero and the argument already ends at the zero polynomial.

#checkpoint("8.1", [Why does the theorem not directly apply to a polynomial ring in infinitely many variables?], [The ideals $(x_1) subset (x_1,x_2) subset dots$ form a nonstabilizing chain. The finite-variable induction cannot cover infinitely many steps at once.])
#takeaways[Noetherianity makes subobjects finitely describable. Hilbert's theorem says adjoining finitely many polynomial variables preserves that control.]
