== Lecture 16 · Algebraic structure in geometry and representations
#focus[Use quotients, localization, tensors, and finiteness together in concrete applications.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall exactness of localization, right exactness of tensors, and distinct-root interpolation.


For polynomial equations over a field, the coordinate ring records algebraic relations among coordinate functions. We use this as a motivating algebraic construction; a full equivalence between varieties and rings requires additional hypotheses and belongs to a later algebraic-geometry course.

#example[Two intersecting axes][Let $A=K[x,y] slash(x y)$. The residue classes of $x,y$ are nonzero but their product is zero. Localizing at powers of $x$ makes $x$ a unit and forces $y=0$, so $A_x iso K[x,x^(-1)]$. This describes the part of the horizontal axis away from the intersection. The formula defines inverse homomorphisms: send $y$ to zero and retain $x$ and its inverse.]

#strategy[Express relations through quotients, invert only declared scalars, and use interpolation idempotents to split an operator.]

#theorem[Finite presentations over Noetherian rings][Every finitely generated module over a Noetherian ring is finitely presented.]
#proof[Choose a surjection $R^n -> M$. Its kernel is a submodule of the Noetherian module $R^n$, hence is generated by finitely many elements. Mapping a finite free module onto that kernel gives a presentation $R^m -> R^n -> M ->0$.]

In a computation, this means finitely many generators have finitely many generating relations, even though there are infinitely many consequences of those relations. It does not mean every algorithm will be fast, nor that a specific chosen relation list is already complete.

#example[Specializing a module][For an $R$-module $M$ and maximal ideal $frak(m)$, the tensor $M otimes_R R slash frak(m)$ is $M slash frak(m)M$, a vector space over the residue field. A matrix presentation can therefore be specialized by reducing each entry modulo $frak(m)$. Right exactness ensures the resulting cokernel is the specialized module.]

#theorem[Diagonalization of a finite-order operator][Let $T^n=1$ on a finite-dimensional $K$-space. If $t^n-1$ splits into distinct linear factors over $K$, then the space is the direct sum of the eigenspaces of $T$.]
#proof[Let its distinct roots be $lambda_1,dots,lambda_n$. Lagrange interpolation gives polynomials $e_i$ with $e_i(lambda_j)=delta_(i j)$. Modulo $t^n-1$, they satisfy $sum_i e_i=1$, $e_i e_j=0$ for $i!=j$, and $(t-lambda_i)e_i=0$. Substitute $T$ to obtain mutually orthogonal projections summing to the identity, with images in the corresponding eigenspaces. Conversely $e_i(T)$ acts as identity on its own eigenspace and zero on the others. These projections give the direct-sum decomposition.]

The distinct-root assumption holds when the characteristic does not divide $n$ and all $n$th roots of unity lie in $K$. In characteristic dividing $n$, nontrivial nilpotent behavior can occur instead. This links the field-theory derivative criterion to the module theory of a group action.

#example[Characteristic changes an operator decomposition][Over a field of characteristic two, let $N=mat(0,1;0,0)$ and $T=1+N$. Then $N^2=0$ and $T^2=1+2N+N^2=1$, but $T$ is not the identity and is not diagonalizable: its only eigenvalue is one, so a diagonalizable such matrix would be the identity. The polynomial $t^2-1=(t-1)^2$ has a repeated root.]

This checks the exact hypothesis in the interpolation proof. Distinct roots make denominators $lambda_i-lambda_j$ invertible and supply separate projections; repeated roots remove that construction and allow nilpotent parts. The field theory and module theory are describing the same obstruction.

#checkpoint("8.2", [What does tensoring a presentation with a residue field preserve, and what may it lose?], [It preserves the cokernel description and right exactness, but an originally injective map may become noninjective. Relations can specialize to zero.])

